To assist the reader I have added the names assigned to the ecclesiastical ¶ by Glarean, which were wrongly distributed among the scales owing to his ¶ the older tonal modes with the later (transposed) minor Greek scales, ^i ¶ which are more known among musicians than the proper Greek names. But ¶ shall not use Glarean's names without expressly mentioning that they refer to an ¶ mode. It would be really better to forget them altogether. The old ¶ notation of Ambrose was much more suitable, but as his figures have ¶ altered again and do not suffice for all modes, I have ventured to propose a ¶ nomenclature in the above table, which will save the reader the trouble of ¶ the systems of Greek names, of which Glarean's are certainly wrong, ¶ the others are also perhaps not quite correctly applied. The principle of the ¶ nomenclature is this. By 'the mode of Fourth of C,' is meant a mode of ¶ C is the tonic, but which has the same signature (or additional ji and \) ¶ as the major scale formed on the Fourth of the diatonic scale beginning ¶ C; that is on F. The minor Seventh, minor Third, minor Si.xth, and minor ¶ must always be understood as the intervals intended in this case.f If the ¶ intervals were selected the tonic would not occur in their scales. Thus ^ ¶ the mode of the minor Third of C" is the scale with the tonic C, having the ¶ of E\) major (that is B\}, E\), A\)), because E\) is the minor Third of C; ¶ is therefore C minor, at least as it is played in the descending scale. I hope ¶ reader will have no difficulty in understanding what is meant by this notation. "{:

This was the tonal system in the best times of Greek art, up to the Macedonian ¶ Airs were at first limited to a tetrachord, as is still often the case in the ¶ Catholic liturgy. They were afterwards extended to an Octave. Longer ¶ were not necessary for singing, as the Greeks refused to employ the straining ¶ notes, and unmetallic deep notes of the human voice. Modern Greek songs, ¶ which Weitzmann has made a collection,^ have also a surprisingly small com¶ If Phrynis (victor in the Panathenaic competitions, B.C. 457) added a ninth ¶ to his cithara, the chief advantage of the arrangement was to allow of ¶ from one kind of scale to another.

¶ The later Greek scale, which first occurs in Euclid's works of the third century ¶ embraces two Octaves, thus arranged:

the former table under this name, but really the Hypolydian (4), c 204 d 182 c^ 204 /i| 112 ij 182 ffj 204 h^ 112 c Refer to the Duodenarium, App. XX. ¶ E. art. \^.— Translator.']

* [If we subtract each of the numbers in ¶ names of the modes here proposed, from ¶ (reckoning 1 as 8 its Octave), we obtain the ¶ on p. 267f, which shew the number of ¶ note in the major scale determined by the ¶ on which the special scale begins. ¶ as 9 less 7 is 2, the mode of the minor ¶ is that numbered 2 on pp. 267c, 268f. ¶ we call the major scale, when reduced to a ¶ form, 1. do, 2. re, .3. mi, 4. f%, ¶ so, 6. la, 7. ti, then these transformed modes

¶ may be called with the Tonic Sol-faists the re, mi, &c., modes respectively. — Translator.']

t [The qualification minor will therefore be always used in this translation, and has been inserted in the above table. — Translator.']

\ [In App. XX. sect. E. No. 10, I have endeavoured to deduce scales for harmonic use, ¶ from a general theory of harmony which termines the precise value of each tone as part of a chord, and I have given precise names for them, there exemplified. This harmonic deduction of scales is quite indejiendent of the historical melodic deduction in the text. —Translator.]

